<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=electrolysis">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Electrolysis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1800">1800</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Electrolysis is the use of a direct electric current to drive a chemical reaction that would not otherwise occur, as in separating elements from ores and compounds. In 1800 William Nicholson and Anthony <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a>, furthering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volta-river">Volta</a>’s experiments, placed the wires of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaic-pile">voltaic pile</a> in water and saw hydrogen and oxygen bubble from them. Humphry Davy discovered potassium, sodium, barium, calcium and magnesium by electrolysis by 1808, and his assistant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-faraday">Michael Faraday</a> published two laws of electrolysis and introduced the word in 1834. The method later yielded gallium in 1875, fluorine in 1886 and, through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-heroult-process">Hall–Héroult process</a> of the same year, aluminium.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word electrolysis was introduced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-faraday">Michael Faraday</a> in 1834, from the Greek for amber, long associated with electrical phenomena, and for dissolution. The technique itself is older. In 1785 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> scientist Martin van Marum used an electrostatic generator to reduce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, zinc and antimony from their salts, without knowing what he was doing. In 1789 Adriaan Paets van Troostwijk and Jan Rudolph Deiman produced small amounts of hydrogen and oxygen from water in the same way. Luigi Galvani's experiments with frog legs in 1791 led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-volta">Alessandro Volta</a> to test his claims, which gave insight to Humphry Davy's ideas. In 1800 William Nicholson and Anthony Carlisle, with Johann Ritter also credited, showed the first sustained electrolysis of water using a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaic-pile">voltaic pile</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Electrolysis passes a direct current through an electrolyte, a substance that contains free ions, producing chemical reactions at the electrodes and decomposing the material. It needs an electrolyte, two electrodes and an external power source, and a partition may keep the products apart. Positive ions move to the negative cathode and negative ions to the positive anode, where electrons are removed, which chemistry calls oxidation, and added, called reduction. Faraday's two laws state that the quantity of product is proportional to the current and that, with cells connected in series, the products are proportional to their equivalent weights. Graphite, called plumbago in Faraday's time, and platinum were chosen for anodes that should not react.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Davy's use of the method led him to discover potassium, sodium, barium, calcium and magnesium in 1807 and 1808. William Thomas Brande isolated lithium by electrolysis in 1821. Paul Émile Lecoq de Boisbaudran found gallium in 1875, producing 3.4 mg. Henri Moissan prepared fluorine on 26 June 1886 and showed it to the Académie des sciences two days later, after many chemists, including Paulin Louyet and Jérôme Nicklès, had died trying to isolate it.</p><p>In 1886 Charles Martin Hall of America and Paul Héroult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> both filed for patents on electrolytic aluminium, Héroult in May and Hall in July. Hall obtained his patent by proving with letters to his family that his method predated the French filing, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-heroult-process">Hall–Héroult process</a> cut the price of aluminium from four dollars to thirty cents a pound. The Castner–Kellner process for sodium hydroxide followed in 1890, and a Polish patent for electrolysis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and zinc was granted to Stanisław Łaszczyński in 1902. The modern chlor-alkali process, the electrolysis of brine, was developed in 1930 and supplies most of the chlorine and sodium hydroxide that industry needs. Electroplating deposits thin films of metal for decoration and use.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Electrolysis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1800</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>William Nicholson · Anthony Carlisle · <a href="/encyclopedia/michael-faraday">Michael Faraday</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>In 1785 Martinus van Marum reduced tin, zinc and antimony from their salts with an electrostatic generator without knowing the process, and in 1789 Adriaan Paets van Troostwijk and Jan Rudolph Deiman split small amounts of water; Johann Ritter also demonstrated it in 1800.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/voltaic-pile">Electric battery<span>1800</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/hall-heroult-process">Hall–Héroult process<span>1886</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=electrolysis">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
